mother tells us he was killed by  soldiers during protests  in the capital kampala last november he  was 15 years old  and had gone with her to the family's  shop in the city  when news of opposition leader bobby  wines arrest triggered a riot  he was hit by two bullets as they  hurried home more than 50 other people  also died that day  katya manawaya he fell on the ground and  stopped moving  he did not even call out mum for me to  help him i knew he was dead and right  there  i just collapsed well wishes took us to  hospital  but my son was already gone the un and  human rights organizations have blamed  security forces  for using excessive force while breaking  up protests  several opposition candidates including  bobby wine  and patrick amuryat seen being arrested  by police in this video  are challenging president johary  museveni's bid  for a sixth time in office but they have  been blocked from campaigning  and detained multiple times many of our  people lived including candice have been  incarcerated  diagnosed gone through harsh treatment  while museveni has a free ride in every  part of the country that he's  campaigning  government officials insist that  opposition protests are violent  and the leaders have been defying covet  19 regulations  people are burning ties people are  throwing immortals people are addressing  women people erecting roadblocks and  extracting  money people listen listen  people are bringing trucks trippers  and offloading boulders on the road to  block roads  and you say they are peaceful election  violence is not new to uganda  some people however say the stakes are  higher now because more people want  change  the next few days are crucial security  forces have been deployed heavily  in hot spots like this one where  protests by opposition supporters are  common many people we've talked to are  afraid  that if the presidential result is  contested  then things will just get worse hajjara  nakitu tells us she is closing her shop  and moving out of kampala until she can  be sure her only daughter will be safe  there  and she says she has no intention of  voting  in an election she blames for her son's  death  catherine soy al jazeera kampala
 

